ooks like rendered  in the mylab currently  we have a static image and a multiple  choice  selection for students this is just not  nearly as  interactive as the experience they were  just having in the e-text  and doesn't encourage conceptual deep  understanding of the concept so let's  take a look at what this question could  look like  using silva here we have the same  concept  illustrated only now students are  interactively asked to graph  f prime by moving these locator points  these types of questions compared to  what we just saw in the mylab with the  multiple choice really drive home the  concept of the tangent line  and slope to students more than a  multiple choice question  ever could let's look at another example  here we are back in the mathematica  e-text and this time we're in chapter  5 learning about area functions so once  again  students are presented with narrative  and the opportunity  to see the concepts in action using  these interactive figures  to essentially learn by doing back in  the mylab this  is an example a representative example  of the types of exercises that would be  available  for this concept so we have a static  figure  where students are asked to find and  graph the area function  which they would need to do on their own  piece of paper  and enter a free response  here followed by  a multiple choice piece where they're  matching  now let's take a look at what this looks  like as a silver exercise  here students are given the chance to  interact and create the graph  by moving these locator points once  again  they're able to see an immediate impact  of  their actions on the graph  they're invited to play around with it  and are all in all more engaged and  hopefully  obtaining some of that elusive  conceptual understanding that is a  consistent  request from instructors you'll notice  that this is  um placed into a pearson ui so this  exercise would sit along others in  a student's homework they're able to  check their answer  receive immediate feedback  and once they're satisfied with their  answer  they would save and submit this would be  scored alongside  all of those hundreds and thousands of  exercises that we currently have in the  my lab  so you would as an instructor you would  be able to create an assignment that  did have some skill and drill exercises  perhaps a video assignment  mixed in with some of these interactive  style questions  some skill maintenance style questions  so you could devise an assignment  that was more than just drilling the  skills from a particular section  you would give students the opportunity  to watch a little  do a little learn a little  thanks chelsea we are really excited to  collaborate with pearson  to make math exercises more engaging  it's great to see what the psn author  has already achieved with silva  and today i want to show you a few more  insights on how it works how we set up  questions and grading  let me start by highlighting four main  features silva offers to authors  number one is interactivity  let's look at the example we've seen  earlier  here the authors took full advantage of  all the powerful features mathematica  offers  such as creating dynamic interfaces  in combination with graphs  and with that they created a truly  interactive experience for the students  as they move the locators the slope  of the black lines would immediately  adjust  this is interactivity  now imagine a student would like to  exercise a little more  then it would be really boring if they  would always get the exact same  question and for that  we offer parametrization and  randomization  that is creating different  but similar versions of one and the same  question  here the task is the same but the  numbers change  the graph is mirrored and so on  this is parametrization and  randomization  number three is the autograding  let's try to solve this question  as i move the locator i see here now  the black line is tangent  and as we know the derivative  of a quadratic function  is a linear function so let me  try to get this right  all right let's check our answer  and it's correct we get  an additional explanation  you found the derivative function for  gradient quadratic function the  derivative is  always a linear function now this is  autograding  what is really important for the  learning experience  are the cases where the student doesn't  get it right  so imagine they would forgot  to move the first locator  let's try this  now they get a feedback that is  incorrect  but with a tailored explanation  the first point is not correct yet make  sure the black line is tangent  to the blue curve and that you get a  straight red line  now let's do this  and now we see third case it's  created as partially correct so it's  almost right  but outside of the error tolerance we  defined  and of course that can be fully  customized  that's number four tailored feedback  and now you may wonder how do we create  such persons  etc well we want to equip all educators  with some mathematical knowledge with a  tool  that they can create such questions  and for this purpose we created creo  creo is silva's authoring tool  it's an add-on to mathematica and once  you install it  it will be available through the  palettes menu  it takes care of all notebooks and files  and questions and so on  so that you don't need to worry about it  now let's look at the notebook that  created the question we have seen  earlier  number one is graph an area function  here you can see that once  the dynamic content gets a little more  deep  the code also will get a little longer  here is the second example we have seen  a little bit shorter  and here you also see the grading  but let's start with something easy  something from scratch  when you create a new question it will  just look like this  so assume you started with prototyping  in the mathematica notebook  here we have a dynamic module that  includes  a simple locator pane some disk  and now we want to turn this into a  question  how can we do that  we simply copy and paste the code  into creo we want  that it's an interactive question  so copy and paste  that's it now what kind of question  could we ask about it  we could some say something like  find the green disk  okay and now we need to look at the  grading  here we defined a point  and this is dynamic so this will be our  locator later  so what we can do is just say  the point  needs to be smaller than  0.5 and since we are interested in the x  coordinate  for the first on that all right  now let's preview this  so here we go we have a question  find the green disk and we can move the  locator  and point it here to the green disk  and we will get a correct  now if we do this again  we get the exact same question  and if we don't get it right  it will simply be incorrect  okay now let's try to  inject the parametrization here  in this case will be easy to just  use different colors so  let me replace the green with color  and add the parameter we call this  parameter color  and make it  a custom list of colors  red blue green and so on  now we want to inject this into  our interactive interface  so we replace the green by  template slot  and then simply color  now in order that  those colors get recognized  we add the capitalize and to  expression in front all right  let's test this now  we see that we get immediately a  different version  so now it picked pink one and created  the nice color this  so let's try another version  and we get the blue one  so we injected the parametrization  and randomization but we still have  on the kind of black white grading  either you get it right or you get it  wrong  so let's add a few more  conditions here  all right now we can test our question  again  we get a different version and let's  see what we get when we get somewhere in  the middle  now this is considered partially correct  and  the rating works like this if you get  the red one  you get four points and for each disk  you're awake  you get one point deduction  okay now we've set up  a question with parametrization with  partial grading  but let's look at the feedback now  we have two fields one is the solution  one is the explanation field  the solution is given when  you either did not get it right after a  couple of attempts  or when you get it right at some attempt  in this case we could just type  something like  well done but that would be a very  static message  so instead we can toggle here  and use a expression  so we could say this  is and then pick the right color  disk and we could even show it all right  let's test this  now move here  get it correct and this is  the right disk okay  let's look at the explanation now  remember this is for the case when the  student doesn't get it right  so what we could do here  is just saying  something like you did not find the  right disk but we can go even further  so here we can say you haven't found  the right disk yet and then we can  give tailored feedback by taking into  account  the answer they provided so in this case  we can extract from the given answer the  point  and as we know this is a coordinate we  take the first  the x value again rounded and this gives  us  the number of disks they have  away from the actual solution  let's try this  okay so i move it here in the middle  and this would be  three this away and we get you haven't  found the blue disk yet  move the locator three disk further to  the left  and this is how we can provide tailored  feedback  based on the answer the student provided  and now you have seen all the main  features  in a very simplified way so let me recap  we were able to create different  versions of a question by inserting a  parameter  we randomized the color  based on the list we provided  we set up partial grading  so that each step or  each state could be distinguished  and we provided individualized and  tailored feedback  based on the question based on 