 responses so students use  palettes  in the mylab to enter expressions in a  particular way and the system scores  them very rigidly  on what they entered so  by using creo it allows us to leverage  the linguistic  knowledge of wolfram alpha to interpret  student input to exercises  so consider a randomly generated  exercise where the answer  is sine x the parentheses x  the following inputs in silver would be  accepted as correct  of course sine parentheses x sine with a  capital s  parentheses x sign square brackets x  sine space x sine x no parentheses  so eric did this using  a mathematica command to interpret the  student answer into a precise expression  and then compared it with the correct  answer that has been computed into the  code  so expressions randomly generated  correct answers computed by mathematica  student input parsed using wolfram  linguistic knowledge  and interpreted student answer compared  to the correct answer  and at last credit given in the mylab  this is a seemingly small piece  of the puzzle but this represents a real  improvement for students who can  sometimes feel defeated by the tool  when they've truly understood the  underlying concept it can be  discouraging to be marked incorrect  or a small error like that so bringing  this  the the linguistic knowledge uh to bear  on these exercise types will will be  felt immediately by students and  instructors  so i've really focused on calculus but a  pilot is also underway at pearson  for pre-calculus for linear algebra  and differential equations and the  authors of those titles are equally  enthusiastic about silva's potential  as it will help them bring their  first-rate content from their books  to life for students within the mylab

